# THE SIFREI ROWS on Numbers 30 (THE NUMBERS WALK sitting 10 — THE VOWS, 2026-09-12): piskaot 153-156 by POSITION — the four between 152
# (29:39) and 157 (31:1); SEVENTEEN rows at the shelf's row grain, each opened and verdicted; the middah named where the row argues by one
# (logic/MIDDOT.md). NO piska on 30:1 (152:1's, read at sitting 9). The rows' own defects (the seventh mistyped head, a citation and a
# speaker wrong, the English supplying the Mishnah, a name and a word garbled in the Hebrew, an attribution, a conclusion and a lemma dropped)
# read to their verses (RESEARCH_LOG.md).
from vows_ink import *
S = {}
def srow(p, r, verdict, prose): S[(p, r)] = (verdict, prose)

srow(153, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"AND MOSES SPOKE TO THE HEADS OF THE TRIBES" — WHY? THE TRUMPETS\' TWO VERSES (10:3-4) LEFT THE PRINCES\' GATHERING-PLACE UNSAID; THE IDENTITY "BLOWING"-"BLOWING" BRINGS THEM TO THE TENT\'S DOOR — AND "TO THE HEADS OF THE TRIBES" FIXES THE ORDER: THE HEADS BEFORE THE PEOPLE, IN EVERY SPEECH; R. YONATAN: EXODUS 34:31-32 ALREADY SAYS IT (THE PRINCES RETURN FIRST, THEN ALL ISRAEL) — SO THE PHRASE TEACHES THAT VOWS ARE RELEASED BY EXPERTS ALONE. The row reads the frame: the identity of expression (I2) "blowing" at the congregation and "blowing" at the princes (10:3-4, cut on the DB: "and they shall gather to you" at both) supplies the door of the tent; "or is the first in Scripture the first in act?" — 30:2 answers: the heads first, and since Moses\' speeches are written unqualified, the one specified case governs them all (I3\'s form); R. Yonatan makes the frame free by finding the order at Exodus 34:31-32 ("and there returned to him Aaron and all the princes of the congregation ... and afterward all the children of Israel drew near" — cut) — the FREED phrase then teaches "the release of vows is by experts alone" (the sage\'s absolution — a rule the ink of the chapter never states: the Sifrei\'s own labeled addition, the data channel). THE INK: "the heads of the tribes" three Bible seats — this and Solomon\'s assembly (1 Kings 8:1, 2 Chronicles 5:2); "TO the heads" one (computed). The middot: I2 (the identity "blowing"), the unqualified-and-specified rule, and the freeing of a phrase for a second law (R. Yonatan). The exam: Nedarim 78a (the release of vows by experts — "to the heads of the tribes"), Chagigah 10a (the release of vows "flies in the air" — no scriptural ground but this), Mishnah Nedarim 9:1-10.')
srow(153, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"THIS IS THE THING" — MOSES PROPHESIED WITH "THUS SAID THE LORD" AS THE PROPHETS DID (EXODUS 11:4) AND ADDED "THIS IS THE THING"; AND: THE HUSBAND ANNULS, THE SAGE DOES NOT; THE SAGE PERMITS, THE HUSBAND DOES NOT — TWO A-FORTIORI ARGUMENTS REFUSED BY THE WORDS. The formula "this is the thing" read twice: as the mark of Moses\' prophecy beyond the prophets\' (Exodus 11:4 "and Moses said: thus said the LORD" — cut), and as a LIMITER: two a-fortiori arguments would let the husband permit (he who annuls, all the more permits) and the sage annul (he who permits, all the more annuls) — "it is therefore written \'this is the thing\'": each has his own verb. THE INK: the formula eight Bible seats, and the two Numbers seats (30:2, 36:6) are the book\'s two law chapters without a divine frame (computed); the husband\'s verb in the chapter is "annul" (six tokens), "permit" never — the sage\'s office is the Sifrei\'s, not the ink\'s (computed on the roots). The middot: E-class limiter ("this" excluding), two refused a-fortiori (I1 turned back by the verse — MIDDOT.md\'s "the verse refuses the inference"). The exam: Nedarim 77b-78a, Chagigah 10a, Ketubot 74b.')
srow(153, 3, 'MATERIAL', '"A MAN, IF HE VOW A VOW" — "A MAN" EXCLUDES THE MINOR; BUT ALSO THE BOY OF THIRTEEN AND A DAY? THE IDENTITY "VOW"-"VOW" WITH THE NAZIRITE\'S (6:2) SETTLES IT — HIS VOWS STAND; "VOWS A VOW" — THE VOW MUST LEAN ON A VOWED THING ("LIKE AN OFFERING"), OR IS "TO THE LORD" REQUIRED? "TO BIND A BOND" — IN ANY EVENT; VOWS AGAINST OATHS: A VOW IS AS BY THE KING\'S LIFE, AN OATH AS BY THE KING HIMSELF (2 KINGS 2:2); "ON HIS SOUL" — HE BINDS HIMSELF, NOT OTHERS: R. ELIEZER\'S A-FORTIORI (HE MAY ANNUL HER VOWS BEFORE SHE MAKES THEM) REFUSED. The minor excluded from "a man"; the boy of thirteen and a day kept by the identity of expression (I2) with 6:2 — "when he clearly utters to vow a vow" (the ink: "clearly utters" four Bible seats — Leviticus 27:2, Numbers 6:2, Deuteronomy 17:8, Zechariah 8:6; computed) — THE TWO FILES DIFFER ON THE IDENTITY\'S CONTENT: the English reads it as the nazirite verse\'s own verb ("ki yafli" — the distinct utterance) and adds "if he were twelve years and one day old, his vows are examined"; the Hebrew row reads "a vow with a freewill offering beside it" and has no "examined" clause — the translator supplying the Mishnah (Niddah 5:6) and the Talmud\'s reading (RESEARCH_LOG.md); the vow\'s "support" (leaning on a vowed object) against the reading that "to the LORD" must be said — "to bind a bond" in any event (the Hebrew\'s מכל מקום, "in any case"); the difference between vows and oaths — the king\'s life against the King (the citation: the Hebrew "2 Kings 2", the words "as the LORD lives and as your soul lives, I will not leave you" at 2 Kings 2:2, 2:4, 2:6 and 4:30 (computed) — THE ENGLISH CITES "II Kings 4:20" AND GLOSSES "(King David)": no such verse carries the words and David never speaks them — Elisha to Elijah, the Shunammite to Elisha; RESEARCH_LOG.md); "on his soul" — R. Eliezer\'s a-fortiori (if he may annul his own vows before he makes them where he cannot after, then his wife\'s before she makes them, where he can after) turned back by "on his soul". THE INK: "he vows a vow" one seat, "to bind a bond" one seat, "on his soul" the chapter\'s one masculine soul-token against ten "her soul" (computed). The middot: I2 (the identity with 6:2), the "in any event" reading of the second clause, the refused a-fortiori (I1). The exam: Niddah 45b-46a (the ages), Nedarim 10a-13b (the vow\'s support — "like an offering"), Shevuot 20a (vows against oaths — "by the king\'s life"), Nedarim 75a-b (annulling vows before they are made — R. Eliezer against the sages).')
srow(153, 4, 'MATERIAL', '"TO BIND A BOND ON HIS SOUL" — BIND THE PERMITTED, NOT PERMIT THE FORBIDDEN (NO OATH TO EAT CARRION); "ON HIS SOUL" — THE INWARD ACCEPTANCE COUNTS; "HE SHALL NOT PROFANE HIS WORD" — NOT MAKE IT PROFANE: THE SAGE MAY NOT RELEASE HIMSELF; TWO TRANSGRESSIONS — "HE SHALL NOT PROFANE" AND "YOU SHALL NOT DELAY" (DEUTERONOMY 23:22); R. ELIEZER AND R. AKIVA EQUATE THE UTTERANCE WITH THE OATH; "AND A WOMAN" — LIKENED TO THE MAN; IN HER YOUTH: NOT THE MATURE, NOT THE MINOR — TWELVE YEARS AND A DAY; "A BOND" IS AN OATH (30:11); "HER FATHER\'S HOUSE" — HIS DOMAIN: THE WIDOW OF BETROTHAL INCLUDED, THE WIDOW OF MARRIAGE EXCLUDED BY "IN HER YOUTH IN HER FATHER\'S HOUSE". The row runs 30:3 to 30:4: "to bind" limits the oath to binding what is permitted; "on his soul" brings the vow accepted inwardly (the Hebrew: "received upon himself by vow or oath"); "he shall not profane his word" — the sage releases others, not himself (an a-fortiori refused: "if he releases others, should he not release himself?"); the two transgressions — the delay ban of Deuteronomy 23:22 (the ink: "when you vow a vow to the LORD your God you shall not delay to pay it" — cut; the vow-verb form "she/you vow a vow" at Deuteronomy 23:22, Ecclesiastes 5:3 and 30:4 alone; computed); R. Eliezer and R. Akiva on the equation of utterance with oath — R. Akiva from "according to all that proceeds from his mouth"; "and a woman" — the woman likened to the man (the E-class likening by adjacency: both transgress both); "in her youth" — past minority, short of maturity — the sages\' twelve years and a day (THE ENGLISH ADDS the identity with 6:2 and "those of a girl of eleven are examined" — the Hebrew row carries neither; RESEARCH_LOG.md); the vow\'s support again; "a bond" is an oath from 30:11 ("or bound a bond on her soul BY AN OATH" — the chapter\'s own gloss; computed); "her father\'s house" — her father\'s DOMAIN: widowed or divorced from betrothal, she is still his; "in her youth in her father\'s house" — ALL her youth in his house: the widow of marriage excluded. THE INK: "in her youth" two seats, both here; "her father\'s house" seven Bible seats, the priest\'s daughter\'s return "as in her youth" among them (computed). The middot: E-class limiters, the refused a-fortiori, the likening of the woman to the man by adjacency, the age fixed by two exclusions (the Sifrei\'s "how is this resolved?" form), the chapter reading itself (30:11 glossing 30:4). The exam: Shevuot 27a (binding the permitted), Shevuot 26b (the inward oath), Nedarim 81b (the sage not for himself), Rosh Hashanah 4a-6a (the delay), Niddah 45b (the ages), Nedarim 70a-b (the betrothed widow and the father\'s domain).')
srow(153, 5, 'MATERIAL', '"IF HER FATHER HEAR HER VOW" — THE DEAF EXCLUDED; TOLD BY OTHERS COUNTS ("ON THE DAY THAT HE HEARS"); "AND HE BE SILENT TO HER" — HE MUST INTEND HER: "I THOUGHT IT WAS MY WIFE\'S" — HE MAY STILL ANNUL; "THEN ALL HER VOWS SHALL STAND" — A VOW CONFIRMED FOR ONE HOUR CAN NEVER BE ANNULLED. Three limiters on 30:5: the hearing excludes the deaf; the hearing by report from "on the day of his hearing" (30:6 — the Hebrew row proves it from "then all her vows shall stand"); "to her" — the intended daughter; and the standing rule: confirmed once (by silence), never annulled — the verse\'s "shall stand" read as final ("Scripture tells that every vow, if confirmed one hour, may not be annulled"). THE INK: "and her father is silent to her" — the silence-verb\'s form at 30:5 and 30:8 alone; the stand-root twelve tokens in the chapter (computed). The middot: E1-class exclusions (the deaf), the verse pair read for the report (30:6 supplying 30:5), the "how do I uphold both verses?" form resolving 30:5\'s "shall stand" against 30:6\'s "restrain" — the confirmation irreversible. The exam: Nedarim 72b-73a (the deaf; the report), Nedarim 69a-b (confirmed and then annulled — "once confirmed, cannot annul"), Mishnah Nedarim 10:7.')
srow(153, 6, 'MATERIAL', '"AND IF HER FATHER RESTRAIN HER" — WHAT IS RESTRAINT? 30:9 "HE RESTRAINS HER AND ANNULS HER VOW" TEACHES AT THE HUSBAND THAT RESTRAINT IS ANNULMENT AND THAT SILENCE ON THE HEARING DAY IS AS THE VOW\'S DAY; WHENCE THE FATHER? THE INDUCTION FAILS (ANNULMENT HAS AN EXCEPTION, CONFIRMATION NONE), THE A-FORTIORI FROM THE HUSBAND FAILS (HE ANNULS IN HER MATURITY, THE FATHER DOES NOT) — "THESE ARE THE STATUTES ... BETWEEN A MAN AND HIS WIFE, BETWEEN A FATHER AND HIS DAUGHTER" (30:17): THE FATHER LIKENED TO THE HUSBAND; "AND THE LORD WILL FORGIVE HER" — ANNULLED IN HIS HEART, BROKEN BY HER WILLFULLY: SHE NEEDS FORGIVENESS (THE SWINE AND THE LAMB); "BECAUSE HER FATHER RESTRAINED HER" — HIS ACT, NOT HER ASSUMPTION, NOT A CARETAKER\'S (R. YOSHIYAH); R. YONATAN: A MAN\'S MESSENGER IS AS HIMSELF. The Sifrei\'s long engine on one verse: the word "restraint" defined by its pairing with "annul" at 30:9 (the ink: the two verbs adjacent at 30:9 alone; computed); the transfer of the hearing-day rule from the husband to the father tried by induction ("since he may confirm and may annul...") and refused (annulment "has left its class" — the inward annulment is no annulment — confirmation has not), tried by a-fortiori (the husband whose authority never becomes exclusive against the father whose does) and refused (the husband annuls in her maturity), and DECIDED BY THE LIKENING (hekkesh) of 30:17 — "you are compelled to liken the father to the husband" (the Hebrew\'s על כרחך, "against your will"): the first of four rows that end on 30:17; the forgiveness clause read for the woman who broke a vow her father had annulled without her knowing — she needs forgiveness for the intent, and all the more the woman whose vow stood (the a-fortiori, and the parable: one who meant to eat swine and ate lamb needs forgiveness — how much more one who meant to and did); "because her father restrained her" — her assurance "I know father annuls every vow he hears" is no annulment, and a caretaker told "annul her vows till I return" is no father (R. Yoshiyah) — R. Yonatan: "in every place a man\'s messenger is as himself" (the dispute carried, dual-track). THE INK: "the LORD will forgive her" three seats, all here; "because" at 30:6 and 30:15 the chapter\'s two "ki" as "because" (computed). The middot: the word defined by its pair (E-class), the induction and its refutation ("no — this may be true of..."), the a-fortiori refuted by a difference, the likening that decides where reasoning fails (hekkesh-class (the likening by juxtaposition, uncoded among the thirteen — MIDDOT.md\'s own case law) — the hekkesh from a verse that names both), the a-fortiori on the forgiveness, the messenger dispute. The exam: Nedarim 71b-72a (restraint = annulment; the father), Kiddushin 41b-42a (the messenger as the man; the Sifrei\'s R. Yonatan), Nedarim 72b (annulled without her knowledge), Mishnah Nedarim 10:1-4.')
srow(153, 7, 'MATERIAL', '"AND IF SHE BE TO A MAN AND HER VOWS BE UPON HER" — THE BETROTHED (R. YOSHIYAH); R. YONATAN: BOTH — SCRIPTURE SPLITS THE DOMAIN: IN HER FATHER\'S HOUSE HER FATHER AND HER HUSBAND ANNUL, MARRIED HER FATHER DOES NOT; "HER VOWS UPON HER" — THOSE THAT CAME WITH HER FROM HER FATHER\'S HOUSE (THE VOWS IN HIS DOMAIN A-FORTIORI); ONLY VOWS NEVER CONFIRMED OR ANNULLED — THE INDUCTION AND THE A-FORTIORI FAIL, 30:17 LIKENS THE HUSBAND TO THE FATHER; "THE UTTERANCE OF HER LIPS" — AN OATH, AS LEVITICUS 5:4 "TO UTTER WITH THE LIPS". The verse assigned to the betrothed by the standing pair of tannaim (the Hebrew names the second "R. Yochanan" — a garble for R. Yonatan, the Sifrei\'s constant partner of R. Yoshiyah; RESEARCH_LOG.md), the domain split as R. Yonatan\'s alternative (the joint annulment of the betrothed maiden — 156:3\'s R. Yishmael); "her vows upon her" read for the vows brought from the father\'s house, the vows made in the husband\'s domain a-fortiori; the confirmed-or-annulled vows excluded by the same three-step engine as 153:6 (induction refused: the father annuls only the unconfirmed but does not annul in her maturity; a-fortiori refused; "not having merited it by reasoning — Scripture: these are the statutes") — THE HEBREW\'S CONCLUSION READS "so THE FATHER annuls only the unconfirmed" where the target is the husband (the English right; a garbled word — RESEARCH_LOG.md); "the utterance of her lips" = an oath by the identity with Leviticus 5:4\'s "to utter with the lips" (the ink: the utterance-noun two seats, both here; the oath-verb\'s "to utter" one seat, Leviticus 5:4; computed). The middot: the assignment of a verse to a case by the other verse (30:11 takes the married, so 30:7 is the betrothed — E-class "what does the verse come to teach?"), the a-fortiori (I1), the likening (hekkesh-class (the likening by juxtaposition, uncoded among the thirteen — MIDDOT.md\'s own case law)) deciding, I2 (the identity "utter"). The exam: Nedarim 66b-67b (the betrothed maiden — father and husband together), Nedarim 70a (the vows brought from the father\'s house), Shevuot 20a (utterance = oath), Mishnah Nedarim 10:1.')
srow(153, 8, 'MATERIAL', '"AND HER HUSBAND HEAR" — THE DEAF EXCLUDED; BY REPORT — FROM "ON THE DAY HER HUSBAND HEARS" (30:9); "AND HE BE SILENT TO HER" — INTENDING HER ("I THOUGHT IT WAS MY DAUGHTER\'S"); "THEN ALL HER VOWS SHALL STAND" — CONFIRMED FOR THE HOUR, NEVER ANNULLED. 153:5\'s three limiters restated at the husband, word for word (the ink: 30:8 repeats 30:5\'s clause with "her husband" for "her father" and "on the day of his hearing" added — computed on the tokens). The middot: as 153:5. The exam: Nedarim 72b-73a, 69a.')
srow(153, 9, 'MATERIAL', '"AND IF ON THE DAY HER HUSBAND HEARS HE RESTRAINS HER AND ANNULS HER VOW" — THE HUSBAND\'S VERSE TEACHES THE TWO RULES: RESTRAINT IS ANNULMENT, AND SILENCE ON THE HEARING DAY IS AS THE VOW\'S DAY. The short row that 153:6 leaned on: 30:9 is the seat where "restrain" and "annul" stand together (the ink: the two verbs adjacent here alone in the Bible; computed) — the definition the father\'s verse borrows. The middah: the word defined by its pair. The exam: Nedarim 71b-72a.')
srow(153, 10, 'MATERIAL', '"AND HE ANNUL HER VOW WHICH IS UPON HER" — THE VOWS UPON HER, NOT THE VOWS SHE WILL MAKE; R. ELIEZER: HE MAY (THE A-FORTIORI FROM HIS OWN VOWS); THE SAGES: "WHICH IS UPON HER" — ONLY THOSE MADE; AND FROM 30:14 "HER HUSBAND SHALL CONFIRM IT AND HER HUSBAND SHALL ANNUL IT" — WHAT CAN COME TO CONFIRMATION CAN COME TO ANNULMENT, WHAT CANNOT, CANNOT; "AND THE LORD WILL FORGIVE HER" — AS ABOVE. R. Eliezer\'s a-fortiori for annulling vows in advance (153:3\'s form) met by the sages with the words "which is upon her"; the second ground from 30:14\'s two verbs on one object — the confirmation and the annulment have the same reach (a vow not yet made cannot be confirmed, so cannot be annulled); the forgiveness clause restated (the Hebrew row breaks off "as above"). THE INK: "which is upon her" at 30:9 (and "which are upon her" 30:15); the two verbs on one object at 30:14 (computed). The middot: E-class restrictor ("which is upon her"), the parallel reach of two verbs (the tradition\'s "what comes into the class of X comes into the class of Y"), the refused a-fortiori. The exam: Nedarim 75a-76b (annulling in advance — R. Eliezer and the sages; "what came to confirmation came to annulment"), Mishnah Nedarim 10:7.')
srow(154, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"AND THE VOW OF A WIDOW OR A DIVORCED WOMAN" — WIDOWED OR DIVORCED FROM MARRIAGE, NOT FROM BETROTHAL: AS THE MATURE DAUGHTER (WHOM HER FATHER CANNOT ANNUL) HAS WHOLLY LEFT HIS DOMAIN, SO THESE; R. AKIVA: "AN ORPHAN IN HER FATHER\'S LIFETIME"; REMARRIED, SHE IS NOT "A WIDOW" — 30:11 "IF IN HER HUSBAND\'S HOUSE" IN ANY EVENT, EVEN A WIDOW TO A HIGH PRIEST, A DIVORCEE OR A RELEASED SISTER-IN-LAW TO A COMMON PRIEST; "IN HER HUSBAND\'S HOUSE" — THE MARRIED (30:7 HAVING TAKEN THE BETROTHED) — R. YISHMAEL. The widow and divorcee of 30:10 fixed as the married woman\'s by the analogy from the mature daughter (both outside the father\'s power); R. Akiva\'s name for her — "an orphan in her father\'s lifetime" (the Mishnah\'s phrase); the remarried widow taken by 30:11 "in any event" — even in a forbidden marriage (the high priest\'s widow, the common priest\'s divorcee); "her husband\'s house" assigned to the married by 30:7\'s having the betrothed — THE HEBREW CLOSES "these are the words of R. Yishmael" AND THE ENGLISH DROPS THE ATTRIBUTION (the sixth defect class — RESEARCH_LOG.md). THE INK: "a widow or a divorced woman" three Torah seats — the high priest\'s forbidden wives (Leviticus 21:14), the priest\'s daughter who returns to her father\'s house "as in her youth" (22:13), and this: the vower\'s widow and the priest\'s in one pair of words (computed); "her husband\'s house" here and Ruth 1:9. The middot: the analogy from the mature daughter (the common ground "outside his domain" — I1\'s cousin, the binyan av), "in any event" (מכל מקום) on 30:11, the assignment of verses to cases by exclusion (30:7 the betrothed, 30:11 the married). The exam: Nedarim 70a-b ("an orphan in her father\'s lifetime" — Mishnah Nedarim 11:10), Yevamot 87a, Mishnah Nedarim 11:9.')
srow(154, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"AND HER HUSBAND HEARD" — THE DEAF EXCLUDED, THE REPORT INCLUDED ("ON THE DAY OF HIS HEARING", 30:13); "AND WAS SILENT TO HER" — THE SILENCE OF CONFIRMATION, NOT THE SILENCE TO VEX (30:15\'S "SILENT, SILENT FROM DAY TO DAY" HAS THAT); "THEN ALL HER VOWS SHALL STAND" — CONFIRMED, NOT ANNULLED AFTER; THE HEBREW\'S CLOSE: SCRIPTURE GIVES LEAVE TO ANNUL ALL THE DAY. The married woman\'s verse (30:12) with the three limiters and a fourth: the chapter\'s TWO SILENCES told apart — 30:12\'s "was silent to her" the silence that confirms, 30:15\'s doubled "silent, silent ... from day to day" the silence to vex (the silence of one who means to annul but delays to grieve her) — the ink\'s two forms assigned two jobs; the close "permission to annul all the day" — THE ENGLISH STOPS AT "SHALL STAND" AND DROPS THE HEBREW\'S CONCLUSION (RESEARCH_LOG.md). THE INK: the silence-verb at 30:5, 8, 12 in the single form and at 30:15 doubled (computed). The middot: the two forms two laws (E10-class — the doubled verb as a second content), the exclusions. The exam: Nedarim 77b-78b (the silence to vex; "the whole day"), Mishnah Nedarim 10:8.')
srow(154, 3, 'MATERIAL', '"ALL THAT PROCEEDS FROM HER LIPS ... SHALL NOT STAND" — TO EXCLUDE THE CARETAKER (THE HEBREW\'S LEMMA, DROPPED BY THE ENGLISH); "HER HUSBAND HAS ANNULLED THEM" — HIS ACT: NOT HER ASSURANCE THAT HE WOULD, NOT THE CARETAKER TOLD TO ANNUL TILL HE RETURNS (R. YOSHIYAH); R. YONATAN: A MAN\'S MESSENGER IS AS HIMSELF; "AND THE LORD WILL FORGIVE HER" — AS ABOVE. 153:6\'s caretaker dispute restated at the husband from 30:13\'s "her husband has annulled them" — the Hebrew row opening on the verse\'s earlier clause "all that proceeds from her lips ... shall not stand — to exclude the caretaker" (THE ENGLISH OPENS AT "her husband has annulled them" — a lemma dropped; RESEARCH_LOG.md). THE INK: "all that proceeds from her lips" one seat and its Deuteronomy pair "what proceeds from your lips you shall keep" (23:24); "her husband has annulled them" the annul-root\'s one perfect with the suffix (computed). The middot: the restrictor (the subject named — E-class), the messenger dispute carried dual-track. The exam: Nedarim 72b (the caretaker — "her husband has annulled"), Kiddushin 41b (the messenger), Mishnah Nedarim 10:5.')
srow(155, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"EVERY VOW AND EVERY OATH OF BINDING TO AFFLICT THE SOUL" — WHY? FROM 30:9 "HE ANNULS THE VOW UPON HER" I MIGHT THINK EVERY VOW: SCRIPTURE SPEAKS OF VOWS OF AFFLICTION; VOWS BETWEEN HIM AND HER — FROM 30:17 "BETWEEN A MAN AND HIS WIFE"; R. YOSHIYAH: AS THE AFFLICTION-VOW IS ONE OTHERS CANNOT RELEASE FOR HER, SO ALL; R. YONATAN: "THE FRUITS OF THE WORLD" HE ANNULS, "OF THIS PROVINCE" HE BRINGS FROM ANOTHER, "OF THIS SHOPKEEPER" HE CANNOT — UNLESS HIS LIVING IS FROM HIM; THE FATHER? "I REASONED AND REVERSED; THE REVERSAL FELL AND I MERITED THE FIRST REASONING" — AND IT TOO FAILS: 30:17 LIKENS THE FATHER TO THE HUSBAND; "HER HUSBAND SHALL CONFIRM IT AND HER HUSBAND SHALL ANNUL IT" — FIGS AND GRAPES: CONFIRMED FOR FIGS, ALL CONFIRMED; ANNULLED FOR FIGS, NOT ANNULLED TILL GRAPES TOO (R. YISHMAEL); R. AKIVA: ANNULLED FOR FIGS, ALL ANNULLED — "CONFIRM IT ... ANNUL IT", A PART IS THE WHOLE; THE SAGE\'S PARTIAL RELEASE RELEASES ALL; ONE VOW AGAINST TWO. The longest row of the four piskaot (5,138 characters): (i) the AFFLICTION FILTER on the husband\'s power — 30:14 restricts 30:9\'s "the vow upon her" to vows of self-affliction, and 30:17\'s "between a man and his wife" adds the vows that touch him; R. Yoshiyah generalizes by the common feature (vows no one else can release for her), R. Yonatan by cases — the fruits of the world (annulled), of the province (he supplies from elsewhere), of this shopkeeper (not annulled, unless he alone feeds the house); (ii) THE FATHER\'S REACH — the induction "as the husband annuls only these, so the father" REVERSED ("as the father annuls every vow, so the husband") and the reversal refuted by "in her youth in her father\'s house" (the Hebrew: דנתי וחלפתי, "I reasoned and reversed" — the Sifrei naming its own move), the a-fortiori refused, and 30:17\'s likening deciding — the third row ending on the footer (the Hebrew breaks off "so the father can" — RESEARCH_LOG.md); (iii) THE PARTIAL ANNULMENT — R. Yishmael: confirmation is whole from a part, annulment only whole; R. Akiva from the two verbs with the one object: "he shall confirm IT ... he shall annul IT" — as a part confirms the whole, a part annuls the whole (the ink: the two verbs with the one suffix at 30:14; computed); the sage\'s partial release releases the whole; and the rule\'s bound — one vow ("figs and grapes") against two vows ("figs — and again grapes"): part confirmed, part annulled. THE INK: "to afflict a soul" — the infinitive\'s two Bible seats, Pharaoh\'s and this; "between a man and his wife" one seat (computed). The middot: E-class restrictor (30:14 on 30:9), the generalization by common feature against the case list (R. Yoshiyah / R. Yonatan), THE REVERSED INDUCTION AND ITS REFUTATION (the Sifrei\'s own vocabulary — MIDDOT.md), the likening deciding (hekkesh-class (the likening by juxtaposition, uncoded among the thirteen — MIDDOT.md\'s own case law)), the part-for-whole on a doubled object (R. Akiva — E-class). The exam: Nedarim 79a-83b (vows of affliction, "between him and her" — the whole ninth and eleventh chapters of the Mishnah), Nedarim 66a-b and Mishnah Nedarim 9:6 (a vow released in part released in whole — the sage\'s partial release; the husband\'s partial annulment the R. Yishmael / R. Akiva dispute the docket will route), Nedarim 87a.')
srow(156, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"AND IF HER HUSBAND BE SILENT, SILENT TO HER FROM DAY TO DAY" — THE SILENCE TO VEX (30:12 HAS THE SILENCE TO CONFIRM); "FROM DAY TO DAY" — TWENTY-FOUR HOURS? "HE HAS CONFIRMED THEM, FOR HE WAS SILENT ON THE DAY OF HIS HEARING" — TILL NIGHTFALL; R. SHIMON BEN YOCHAI: TWENTY-FOUR HOURS — "THE TORAH SAID FROM DAY TO DAY". THE SEVENTH MISTYPED HEAD of the export — the piska is headed "30:14" and its first row quotes 30:15 (the doubled "silent, silent ... from day to day" stands at 30:15 alone; computed; RESEARCH_LOG.md). The two silences assigned (154:2\'s partner); THE DEADLINE DISPUTED: "from day to day" could mean a day\'s span (twenty-four hours from the hearing) — the verse\'s own close "on the DAY of his hearing" fixes the day that ends at night; R. Shimon ben Yochai holds the twenty-four hours, reading "from day to day" as the Torah\'s own words for it (the Hebrew\'s exclamation: "but the Torah said from day to day!") — two recorded settings of the annulment window, the compile\'s clock parameter. THE INK: "from day to day" two Bible seats — this and 1 Chronicles 16:23, whose Psalm parallel (96:2) writes the words with the other preposition; "on the day of his hearing" four seats, all here (computed). The middot: E10-class (the doubled verb a second content), the verse\'s close deciding its head (the Sifrei\'s "it is therefore written" from the same verse), the dispute carried. The exam: Nedarim 76b-77a (the day of hearing — till night, or twenty-four hours; R. Shimon ben Yochai; the Sabbath\'s annulment), Mishnah Nedarim 10:8, Shabbat 157a.')
srow(156, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"AND IF HE ANNUL THEM AFTER HIS HEARING" — AFTER HIS CONFIRMATION (THE HEARING ITSELF 30:15 ALREADY HAS); "THEN HE SHALL BEAR HER INIQUITY" — HE ENTERS IN HER PLACE FOR THE SIN; AND A-FORTIORI: IF ONE WHO CAUSES HIS FELLOW TO STUMBLE TAKES HIS PLACE UNDER THE MEASURE OF PUNISHMENT, WHICH IS SMALL, HOW MUCH MORE UNDER THE MEASURE OF GOOD, WHICH IS GREAT. "After his hearing" freed from its plain sense by 30:15\'s "because he was silent on the day of his hearing" — so it means after his confirming; the husband who annuls a confirmed vow, so that she breaks it thinking it annulled, bears her iniquity — "he is put in her place"; the a-fortiori from the two measures (the tradition\'s standing ratio: the measure of good exceeds the measure of punishment — the exam\'s Sanhedrin 100a-b, Yoma 76a). THE INK: "after his hearing" one seat; "he shall bear her iniquity" one seat — "her iniquity" five Torah seats, the suspected wife\'s "that woman shall bear her iniquity" (5:31) among them (computed); ONKELOS: "he shall receive her guilt" (the debt-word; cut). The middot: the phrase freed by its neighbor (E-class), the a-fortiori from the two measures (I1 — the ratio itself a recorded parameter). The exam: Nedarim 79a (annulled after confirmation — "he shall bear her iniquity"), Sanhedrin 100a (the measure of good), Mishnah Nedarim 10:7.')
srow(156, 3, 'MATERIAL', '"THESE ARE THE STATUTES WHICH THE LORD COMMANDED MOSES, BETWEEN A MAN AND HIS WIFE, BETWEEN A FATHER AND HIS DAUGHTER" — THE FATHER LIKENED TO THE HUSBAND AND THE HUSBAND TO THE FATHER IN EVERY RULE STATED; "IN HER YOUTH, HER FATHER\'S HOUSE" — HER YOUTH IN HIS HOUSE, NOT HER YOUTH IN HER HUSBAND\'S; R. YISHMAEL: THE BETROTHED MAIDEN — HER FATHER AND HER HUSBAND ANNUL HER VOWS TOGETHER. The footer read as the chapter\'s engine — the likening (hekkesh) that ran at 153:6, 153:7 and 155:1 stated as a rule "in every rule we have said", BOTH WAYS; "in her youth, her father\'s house" limiting the father to her youth in his house (the husband\'s power reaching past it); R. Yishmael\'s reading — the verse speaks of the betrothed maiden, whose father and husband annul jointly (153:7\'s R. Yonatan). THE INK: "these are the statutes" three Bible seats, this alone without "and the judgments"; "between a man and his wife" and "between a father and his daughter" one seat each; "in her youth" two seats, both here (computed). The middot: hekkesh-class (the likening by juxtaposition, uncoded among the thirteen — MIDDOT.md\'s own case law) (the hekkesh from the verse naming both parties — the two-way likening), E-class restriction ("in her youth in her father\'s house"). The exam: Nedarim 66b-68a (the betrothed maiden; the father and the husband — "these are the statutes"), Mishnah Nedarim 10:1.')
